Structural equivalence: simulated top level vs. schematic
==========================================================

	make netlist	elaborate digital_top into a structural netlist
	make selftest	check the comparison can detect a real error
	make lvs SCHEMATIC=/path/to/digital_top.spice

Why this exists
---------------
digital_top.v is simulated but not taped out.  The schematic and layout
are taped out but not simulated against the cocotb suite.  Nothing
connects the two, so "the tests pass" says nothing about the thing that
gets fabricated unless the two netlists are shown to be the same.

The claim is split in two, because only one half is mechanisable:

  STRUCTURE  the two netlists instantiate the same cells wired to the
	     same nets.  That is what this directory proves.
  BEHAVIOUR  each behavioural model matches the circuit it stands for.
	     Not provable by netlist comparison, ever.  Discharged per
	     block against ngspice --- see validate/switch_polarity for
	     the pattern, and the bias range checks now built into the
	     bandgap and voltgen models.

The structural half is worth more than it sounds.  Every bug found in
this design so far has been a wiring bug:  the dlhrq RESET_B inversion,
bit_index resolving to x, and the bandgap bias crossing.  The last of
those is exactly what a structural compare catches without anyone
having thought to test for it, which is why selftest.py injects it.

How it works
------------
netgen's Verilog reader is a STRUCTURAL netlist reader, not an RTL
elaborator:  it reads digital_top.v happily until the first generate
loop and then stops.  So yosys does the elaboration first.

	digital_top.v + blackboxes.v
	  -> yosys (hierarchy, proc)		elaborates the generates
	  -> digital_top.struct.v		207 instances, 1588 nets
	  -> netgen lvs vs the xschem .spice

TWO yosys write_verilog passes are needed, and both matter.
"-blackboxes" is exclusive rather than additive:  it writes ONLY the
blackbox modules, and a plain write_verilog writes only the others.
Without the blackbox half netgen has no port list for the leaves, so it
infers each placeholder's pins from the connections it happens to see
-- which is how it came to report an error on one tie-off and stay
silent about twenty-eight others.

yosys rejects "real" ports outright, so the structural view cannot carry
reals at all.  LVS_STRUCTURAL in digital_top.v swaps them for plain
nets.  There are eight such guards and they are listed below so they can
be checked by eye --- that is the entire surface where the simulated and
taped-out views are allowed to differ.

The LVS_STRUCTURAL guards in digital_top.v
------------------------------------------
1. The four shared analog pads.  Behaviourally each pad is TWO ports
   (analog_pinN_in / analog_pinN_out) because a real has no
   high-impedance state, iverilog rejects a real inout, and a real
   cannot have two drivers.  Structurally each is one inout wire, which
   is what the schematic and the layout have.  This is an iverilog
   artifact --- the correct construct is a user-defined nettype with a
   resolution function, or Verilog-AMS wreal, neither of which iverilog
   supports.
2-5. Four blocks of "wire real" declarations become plain "wire".  Same
   net either way;  only what it carries differs.
6. avdd_rail / dvdd_rail.  Behaviourally these convert a 1-bit supply
   net into a voltage;  structurally they are aliases for AVDD and DVDD.
   LVS_STRUCTURAL therefore REQUIRES USE_POWER_PINS, which lvs_top.v
   defines alongside it.
7. The resolving mux on the shared pins.  Structurally the diagnostic
   switch output, the pad and the on-chip distribution are one net, so
   the four nets are simply tied together.  The mux exists only because
   a real cannot have two drivers.
8. pad_not_modelled.  The dedicated per-slot analog pads are not
   exported yet, so structurally they are one dangling net.  LVS WILL
   FLAG unconnected pins on every slot wrapper, and that complaint is
   correct:  the schematic has real pads there.  See "known gaps".

A CONSTANT IS NOT A NET
-----------------------
The remaining guards are not real-vs-wire questions but physical ones,
and they exist because of a hole that had to be measured to be found.

netgen DROPS a connection written as a literal.  It does not warn;  the
pin simply does not take part in the comparison.  So every hard-wired
constant in digital_top was silently outside the check:

	18 x proj_addr[4:0]	the slot address, 90 pins
	10 x SRAM tie-offs	A_DLY, A_BM and the BIST pins, 40 pins
	 2 x dig_out_relay	the top of each daisy chain, 24 pins

The proj_addr case was the serious one.  That five-bit address is the
ONLY thing that distinguishes one user_project_control instance from the
other seventeen -- everything else about them is identical -- so it is
the single connection most in need of checking, and it was the one being
ignored.  LVS reported "Circuits match uniquely" on a netlist with two
slots' addresses swapped:  a chip where every project answered to the
wrong selector would have passed.

Only the daisy-chain tie-off ever produced a complaint, and only by
accident:  netgen had already learned dig_out_relay's width from the 16
instances that connect it to a real bus, so when it met the constant it
tried to split it across 12 pins and failed.  proj_addr is a constant in
all 18 instances, so netgen never learned anything to be inconsistent
with.  An error that depends on a port being used correctly SOMEWHERE
else is not a safety net.

The fix is the same in every case, and it is more physically honest than
what it replaces:  these pins are tied to a supply rail in the
schematic, so the structural view ties them to DVDD/DVSS through tie_hi
and tie_lo.  Behaviourally those two nets are just 1 and 0, so nothing
about the simulation changes.  "make netlist" now prints no constant
connections at all, and selftest.py checks that a swapped slot address
is detected.

CONFIRM AGAINST THE SCHEMATIC, since these are assumptions about what it
will contain:
  - proj_addr on each slot, tied per bit to give 1..18
  - the SRAM's A_DLY high, A_BM all high, and every BIST pin low
  - dig_out_relay low at the top of each chain (24 pins, 12 per chain)

"make netlist" rejects constants
--------------------------------
check_netlist.py fails the build if any instance pin is connected to a
literal, naming each one.  That is the rule netgen should enforce and
currently does not -- see the bug report written up in the scratchpad
notes -- so it is enforced here instead, where a regression in
digital_top.v shows up immediately rather than as a quietly smaller LVS.

Tie the pin to the supply net for ITS domain.  Do not expect any tool to
infer which one:  this chip has AVDD/AVSS at 3.3 V and DVDD/DVSS at
1.2 V, so a bare 1'b0 does not identify a net, and a tool that guessed
could bond a 3.3 V domain pin to the 1.2 V return without saying so.

blackboxes.v
------------
Port-only stubs for all 29 leaves, GENERATED by gen_blackboxes.py from
the real module headers.  Do not hand-edit:  a stub whose pins have
quietly drifted from the module it stands for is worse than no stub,
because yosys would elaborate it without complaint and LVS would then
compare the wrong thing.  Regenerate with

	./gen_blackboxes.py > blackboxes.v

The generator applies two transformations.  A real port becomes a plain
wire, and an analog port becomes "inout" whatever the behavioural model
calls it --- the models are unidirectional because a real cannot have
two drivers, but the devices are not.

housekeeping_top, user_project_control and the SRAM are blackboxes here
because they are separately placed, routed and LVS'd.  At this level
they are correctly opaque.

setup.tcl
---------
Permutes the in/out pins of the three analog switch cells.  Those are
symmetric devices --- which terminal is called "in" is a modelling
convention, not a fact about the layout --- so a swap is not an error.
The cost is real and deliberate:  a genuinely reversed switch connection
will also pass.  That is the right trade ONLY because the device is
symmetric.  The power stages are NOT permuted, because IOVDD_IN comes
from the pad ring and IOVDD_OUT feeds one project, and reversing those
would be a genuine error.

selftest.py
-----------
A comparison that always passes is worse than no comparison, because it
reads like evidence -- and this one did pass on a netlist with two slot
addresses swapped, until the tie-offs above were fixed.  So the harness
is checked against deliberately damaged copies before its verdict is
trusted:

	identity    the netlist against a copy of itself     must MATCH
	crossed     two bias nets swapped                    must FAIL
	addresses   two slots' hard-wired addresses swapped  must FAIL

Both injected errors are real ones.  Add a case here whenever a class of
connection turns out not to be covered;  that is what the address case
is, and it is the reason to keep running it.

Planned, not done yet
---------------------
Two improvements to how the hard-wired constants are represented.
Neither is urgent and both can be spliced in later;  they are recorded
here so the reasoning is not lost.

1.  TIE CELLS instead of a direct connection to the rails.  Connecting a
    gate input straight to power or ground is an antenna and ESD
    liability and is generally poor practice.  The PDK has the cells:

	sg13cmos5l_tiehi (L_HI)		sg13cmos5l_tielo (L_LO)

    and neither appears on the PDK's synth_exclude or pnr_exclude lists,
    so an explicitly instantiated one is kept.  The splice point is
    narrow by construction:  tie_hi and tie_lo are defined in exactly
    ONE place in digital_top.v, and everything else refers to them by
    name.  Replacing the two assigns with two instantiations is the
    whole change.

    Note that one shared tie_hi net feeding all 18 slots plus the SRAM
    is not what a real tie-cell implementation looks like --- the point
    of a tie cell is to keep the charge path short, so they are normally
    placed local to their loads.  How many instances to use is a layout
    decision, not a netlist one.

2.  ROM BLOCKS for the constants, like the 32-bit mask ROM already
    feeding housekeeping:  a 12-bit ROM at the top of each daisy chain
    and a 5-bit ROM per slot address.  This is cleaner than either ties
    or literals, because the constant becomes a real instance that LVS
    compares as an object rather than a pattern of net connections.

    ONE CAVEAT, because it is the same trap this directory already fell
    into, one level down.  If all 18 slots instantiate the SAME ROM cell
    with the same connectivity, LVS at digital_top level cannot tell
    them apart:  18 identical instances wired identically are
    interchangeable in the netlist graph, so slot 5's ROM and slot 12's
    could be swapped and the comparison would still pass.  The address
    would have moved inside the ROM, where it becomes that cell's own
    LVS problem and is no longer checked here at all --- exactly the
    blind spot that the literals had.

    So it has to be 18 distinct ROM cells, slot1_addr_rom ..
    slot18_addr_rom, not one metal-programmed cell used 18 times.  Then
    the cell TYPE carries the address, netgen distinguishes them, and it
    is self-documenting in the schematic.  The layouts are not the
    obstacle they look like:  the same script that via-programs the
    32-bit mask ROM can generate all 18 in one pass.

    Distinct MODULES does not mean distinct FILES.  All 18 can live in
    one file, and the same is true of the 18 slot wrappers, which are
    currently one file each for no reason that matters.  This harness
    does not care either way:  gen_blackboxes.py looks up each module by
    name within whatever file it is pointed at, so consolidating is just
    a matter of pointing the 18 SOURCES entries at the same path.
    Verified, not assumed.

    When the representation changes, add a case to selftest.py that
    swaps two slots' addresses in the NEW form and confirm it still
    fails.  The existing case matches on the tied form;  it exits with
    an error rather than passing when it cannot find that form, so it
    will not silently rot, but it will need replacing rather than
    keeping.

Known gaps this comparison will report
--------------------------------------
- The 40 dedicated per-slot analog pins are not exported from
  digital_top, so every slot wrapper will show unconnected analog_pinN.
  Export them when something needs to drive them.
- The slot wrappers themselves are empty in RTL.  They are blackboxes on
  both sides, so their contents are not compared here at all;  each user
  project is its own LVS.
